{
  "title": "HippoRAG: Neurobiologically Inspired Long-Term Memory for Large Language Models",
  "authors": [
    "Bernal Jim\u00e9nez Guti\u00e9rrez",
    "Yiheng Shu",
    "Yu Gu",
    "Michihiro Yasunaga",
    "Yu Su"
  ],
  "year": 2024,
  "arxivId": "2405.14831",
  "abstract": "HippoRAG is a novel retrieval framework inspired by the hippocampal indexing theory of human long-term memory. It enables deeper and more efficient knowledge integration over new experiences by synergistically orchestrating large language models, knowledge graphs, and the Personalized PageRank algorithm.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "The Core Idea",
      "content": "HippoRAG is inspired by the <strong>hippocampal memory indexing theory</strong>, which proposes that human long-term memory relies on interactions between the neocortex and the hippocampus. The neocortex processes and stores actual memory representations, while the hippocampus holds the hippocampal index, a set of interconnected indices that point to memory units on the neocortex and store associations between them.",
      "visualization": {
        "type": "neural-network",
        "description": "A diagram showing the hippocampal memory indexing theory, with the neocortex and hippocampus interacting to store and retrieve memories.",
